  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Displacement Crises through Integrated Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Global Humanitarian Overview underscores the escalating number of people in need worldwide, which reached over 360 million in 2023. Conflicts, compounded by climate-induced disasters, continue to drive displacement. The projection for 2024 is stark with nearly 300 million people projected to require urgent humanitarian assistance and protection globally. The International Organization of Migration (IOM)&#x2019;s Strategic Plan 2024-2028 places paramount importance on saving lives and protecting people on the move, while simultaneously, seeking to drive innovative solutions to displacement and to address challenges of climate change. The newly established Department of Humanitarian Response and Recovery (DHRR) assumes a pivotal role in achieving the two objectives as outlined in the Strategic Plan. Tasked with capacitating and supporting IOM&#x2019;s field offices and partners, DHRR spearheads principled, effective and accountable crisis responses, including anticipatory actions to address future emergencies. In direct alignment with Strategic Plan, the project is strengthening IOM&#x2019;s humanitarian footprint through improving IOM and partner capacities to deliver integrated humanitarian assistance and protection support and reducing risks and impacts of climate change, conflict and instability for communities affected by or at risk of displacement.</narrative>
  </description>
